From e9b745b150bb01ada98adb479cdd4775dca4b289 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: albanread <albanread@googlemail.com>
Date: Thu, 24 Sep 2026 08:22:17 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] libroot/malloc: thread caches, a page owner map, and a heap
 that overcommits

The Mojo compiler ran 60% slower with 8 threads than with one on an 8-CPU
Prose machine. A sixth of its CPU time went to sleeping and waking on malloc's
pool mutexes, which do not spin: free() of another thread's block locked the
freeing thread's pool, then unlocked and locked each other pool in turn until
it found the owner (findpool). And the pages under the pools were reshaped all
the time: PagesAllocator grew its areas with resize_area for each allocation,
committed them in full, and unmapped freed pages from the middle of areas,
which split them. One compile made 11,855 unmaps and 9,247 resizes, and left
2,414 "heap area" areas, most of them 4-32 KB.

The OpenBSD core stays; the path around it changes, after what glibc and
Darwin's libmalloc do:

- Thread caches (thread_cache.c), as glibc's tcache: each thread keeps the
  small blocks it frees, by size class, and hands them out first, without a
  lock. A miss refills half a class from the thread's pool under one lock,
  taking blocks in bitmap order; a full class gives half back to the pools
  that own the blocks. A per-process key in each cached block catches a
  double free by the same thread; a thread's cache is flushed at its exit.

- A page owner map (wrapper.c): two bytes per allocator page, the owning pool
  and the size class, in tables that overcommit. free() finds a block's owner
  and size without a lock and without searching, as glibc does by masking a
  pointer to its heap and Darwin by a region's trailer. findpool()'s search is
  left for pointers the map does not know.

- 16 KB allocator pages, the native page of Apple silicon, whatever the
  system page is. Blocks up to 8 KB come from pages, in 32 classes; LLVM's
  4 KB slabs are four to a page instead of a region each. OpenBSD's page
  caches keep their sizes in bytes.

- A heap that overcommits (PagesAllocator.cpp): one 64 GB area per heap,
  committed page by page as it is first touched, never resized, split or
  unmapped. Free runs past a limit go back with MADV_FREE, which returns the
  pages and the commitment and keeps the addresses; least recently freed
  first. The limit follows the memory the team recently had in use, at
  least 32 MB, at most 1/64 of the machine's memory. A new allocation
  prefers a free run whose pages are still resident. Only allocations over
  16 GB get an area of their own. Memory that was given back is no longer
  zeroed unless the caller needs zeroes.

- Pools twice the CPUs, 8 to 32; pool and PagesAllocator locks spin briefly
  before sleeping, as Darwin's magazine locks do.

Heap memory is now committed when first touched, as on Linux and macOS, and
as thread stacks already are here.

Measured on Prose, 8 CPUs, with HaikuArmQemu's tools/malloc/mallocbench
(4 million operations a thread):

                                  before       after
  small-block churn, 1 thread     20.0 M/s     88-98 M/s
                     8 threads    0.25 M/s     136-155 M/s
  frees from other threads        62 k/s       16-35 M/s
  realloc growth, 1 thread        743/s        25-37 k/s
                  4 threads       54/s         22-31 k/s

`mojo build` of the standard library check, from a cold cache: one thread
3.25 s -> 2.65-2.8 s; eight threads 5.2 s -> 3.5-3.75 s, with a third of the
page faults and 37-44 areas instead of 2,000-2,900.

Tests: tools/malloc/malloctest (10/10, and Haiku's calloc_test, memalign_test
and signal_in_allocator_test[2]).

